<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<usx version="3.0">
  <book code="GAL" style="id">GALATIANS</book>
  <chapter number="1" style="c" sid="GAL 1"/>
  <para style="s1">Apostolic Greeting </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="GAL 1:1"/>
    Paul, an apostle, not appointed by human beings nor commissioned through human mediation, but commissioned through Jesus Christ and God the Father, the One who brought Him back from the dead,
    <verse eid="GAL 1:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="GAL 1:2"/>
    together with all the brothers and sisters who are with me,
    <verse eid="GAL 1:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">To the churches throughout Galatia:</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="GAL 1:3"/>
    May grace or undeserved favour and peace be given to you from God the Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ,
    <verse eid="GAL 1:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="GAL 1:4"/>
    who willingly handed Himself over on behalf of our sins in order to deliver us from the power of this present corrupt age, in harmony with the purposeful will of our God and Father,
    <verse eid="GAL 1:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="GAL 1:5"/>
    to whom honour belongs without end. Amen.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Apostatizing to a Different “Gospel”</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="GAL 1:6"/>
    I am astonished that you are abandoning so quickly the One who summoned you by the gracious work of Christ and are turning towards something presented as a different gospel,
    <verse eid="GAL 1:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="GAL 1:7"/>
    even though it does not truly exist as another message of good news. Rather, certain people are unsettling you and are attempting to distort the message concerning Christ.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="GAL 1:8"/>
    But even if we ourselves, or a heavenly messenger, were to proclaim a message that contradicts the good news we previously announced to you, that one must be placed under divine judgment.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="GAL 1:9"/>
    Just as we have stated before, so I repeat now: If anyone is announcing to you a message that stands in opposition to what you originally received, that one must be condemned.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="GAL 1:10"/>
    For whose approval am I seeking now, that of people or that of God? Or am I attempting to satisfy people? If my aim were still to win human approval, I would not be serving Christ as His bondservant.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Paul’s Calling</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="GAL 1:11"/>
    For I want you to understand, brothers and sisters, that the good news I proclaimed does not originate from human reasoning.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="GAL 1:12"/>
    I did not receive it from any person, nor was I instructed in it by human teachers; rather, it came through a direct unveiling of Jesus Christ.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="GAL 1:13"/>
    You have heard about my former way of life within Judaism, how I relentlessly persecuted the church of God and sought to annihilate it.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="GAL 1:14"/>
    I was progressing in Jewish tradition beyond many of my contemporaries among my people, driven by an intense devotion to the ancestral traditions passed down to me.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="GAL 1:15"/>
    But when God, who had set me apart before my birth and summoned me through His grace, was pleased
    <verse eid="GAL 1:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="GAL 1:16"/>
    to disclose His Son within me so that I might proclaim Him among the Gentiles, I did not immediately seek counsel from mere human sources,
    <verse eid="GAL 1:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="GAL 1:17"/>
    nor did I travel to Jerusalem to consult those who were apostles before me. Instead, I went away to Arabia and later returned to Damascus.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="GAL 1:18"/>
    Then, after three years had passed, I went up to Jerusalem to become personally acquainted with Cephas, and I remained with him for fifteen days.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="GAL 1:19"/>
    I did not see any of the other apostles, except James, the Lord’s brother.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="GAL 1:20"/>
    In what I am writing to you, I affirm before God that I am speaking truthfully.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="GAL 1:21"/>
    After that, I went into the regions of Syria and Cilicia.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="GAL 1:22"/>
    I was still unknown by sight to the churches of Judea that belong to Christ.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="GAL 1:23"/>
    They only kept hearing it said, “The one who once persecuted us is now proclaiming the faith he formerly tried to destroy.”
    <verse eid="GAL 1:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="GAL 1:24"/>
    And as a result, they were giving glory to God because of what He had done in me.
    <verse eid="GAL 1:24"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="GAL 1"/>
  <chapter number="2" style="c" sid="GAL 2"/>
  <para style="s1">The Acceptance of Paul by the Apostles</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="GAL 2:1"/>
    Then, after a span of fourteen years, I went up again to Jerusalem, accompanied by Barnabas, and I also brought Titus with us.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="GAL 2:2"/>
    I went in response to a divine disclosure, and I laid before them the message of good news that I proclaim among the Gentiles. I did this privately with those recognised as leaders, in order to be certain that my labour, past or present, would not prove to be fruitless.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="GAL 2:3"/>
    Yet not even Titus, who was with me and who is a Greek, was forced to submit to circumcision.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="GAL 2:4"/>
    This matter arose because of false brothers and sisters who had been secretly introduced, who slipped in to observe the freedom we have in Christ Jesus, with the intent of reducing us again to bondage.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="GAL 2:5"/>
    We did not give in to them or yield our ground, not even briefly, so that the truth of the gospel would remain intact for you.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="GAL 2:6"/>
    As for those who were regarded as influential, whatever their standing once was makes no difference to me, since God does not evaluate people by outward status. Those recognised as leaders contributed nothing further to my message.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="GAL 2:7"/>
    Instead, when they recognised that I had been entrusted with the good news for the uncircumcised, just as Peter had been entrusted with the good news for the circumcised,
    <verse eid="GAL 2:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="GAL 2:8"/>
    for the same God who was powerfully at work in Peter for his mission to the circumcised was also at work in me for my mission to the Gentiles,
    <verse eid="GAL 2:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="GAL 2:9"/>
    and when James, Cephas, and John, who were acknowledged as pillars, perceived the grace that had been given to me, they extended to Barnabas and to me the right hand of fellowship. This signified that we should go to the Gentiles, while they would go to the circumcised.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="GAL 2:10"/>
    They made this one request, that we would keep the poor in mind, which was precisely what I was eager to do.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Paul Confronts Peter</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="GAL 2:11"/>
    But when Cephas came to Antioch, I opposed him directly, because his conduct stood condemned.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="GAL 2:12"/>
    Before certain people arrived from James, he had been eating with the Gentiles. But when they came, he began to draw back and separate himself, fearing those who insisted on circumcision.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="GAL 2:13"/>
    The rest of the Jews joined him in this pretence, with the result that even Barnabas was led astray by their hypocrisy.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="GAL 2:14"/>
    When I saw that their behaviour was not aligned with the truth of the gospel, I said to Cephas in front of everyone, “If you, though you are a Jew, live like a Gentile and not like a Jew, how can you compel the Gentiles to live as Jews?”
    <verse eid="GAL 2:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Justification through Faith</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="GAL 2:15"/>
    We are Jews by birth and not sinners from among the Gentiles;
    <verse eid="GAL 2:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="GAL 2:16"/>
    yet we know that a man is not justified or declared righteous by performing the works required by the Law, but through faith in Jesus Christ. For this reason, we also have placed our faith in Christ Jesus, so that we may be justified by faith in Christ and not by works of the Law, because no human being will be justified by works of the Law.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="GAL 2:17"/>
    But if, while seeking to be justified or declared righteous in Christ, we ourselves are found to be sinners, does that make Christ a promoter of sin? Absolutely not.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="GAL 2:18"/>
    For if I reconstruct the system I once dismantled, I demonstrate that I am a lawbreaker.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="GAL 2:19"/>
    For through the Law I died to the Law, so that I might live for God.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="GAL 2:20"/>
    I have been put to death with Christ, and I no longer live for myself; rather, Christ lives within me. And the life I now live in the body, I live by faith in the Son of God, who loved me and willingly gave Himself for me.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="GAL 2:21"/>
    I do not nullify the grace of God, for if righteousness comes through the Law, then Christ’s death had no purpose.
    <verse eid="GAL 2:21"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="GAL 2"/>
  <chapter number="3" style="c" sid="GAL 3"/>
  <para style="s1">By Works of the Law, or By Hearing with Faith?</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="GAL 3:1"/>
    You foolish Galatians, who has deceived you, when before your very eyes Jesus Christ was publicly presented as crucified?
    <verse eid="GAL 3:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="GAL 3:2"/>
    This is the one thing I want to learn from you: Did you receive the Spirit by performing the works demanded by the Law, or by listening to the message with faith?
    <verse eid="GAL 3:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="GAL 3:3"/>
    Are you truly so senseless? Having begun your life with God by the Spirit, are you now attempting to reach completion by human effort?
    <verse eid="GAL 3:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="GAL 3:4"/>
    Have you experienced so much for no purpose, if indeed it has been for no purpose?
    <verse eid="GAL 3:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="GAL 3:5"/>
    So then, does the One who supplies the Spirit to you and performs mighty works among you do so because you keep the Law, or because you hear and respond in faith?
    <verse eid="GAL 3:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="GAL 3:6"/>
    In the same way, Abraham trusted God, and that trust was credited to him as righteousness.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="GAL 3:7"/>
    Therefore, recognise that those who exercise faith are the sons of Abraham.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="GAL 3:8"/>
    And the Scripture, foreseeing that God would justify or declare the Gentiles righteous by faith, announced the good news beforehand to Abraham: “In you all the nations will be blessed.”*
    <verse eid="GAL 3:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="GAL 3:9"/>
    As a result, those who belong to faith are blessed along with Abraham, the believer.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Righteous Shall Live by Faith</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="GAL 3:10"/>
    For all who rely on the works of the Law place themselves under a curse, because it is written: “Cursed is everyone who does not continue to do everything written in the book of the Law.”*
    <verse eid="GAL 3:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="GAL 3:11"/>
    It is clear that no one is justified or declared righteous before God by means of the Law, because “The righteous one shall live by faith.”*
    <verse eid="GAL 3:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="GAL 3:12"/>
    The Law, however, does not operate on the principle of faith; rather, “The one who practises them shall live by them.”*
    <verse eid="GAL 3:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="GAL 3:13"/>
    Christ rescued us from the curse pronounced by the Law by taking that curse upon Himself, for it is written: “Cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree,”*
    <verse eid="GAL 3:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="GAL 3:14"/>
    so that in Christ Jesus the blessing promised to Abraham might extend to the Gentiles, and so that through faith we might receive the promised Spirit.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Law and the Promise</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="GAL 3:15"/>
    Brothers and sisters, I am speaking in familiar human terms: even a covenant established by human agreement, once confirmed, cannot be annulled or amended.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="GAL 3:16"/>
    Now the promises were spoken to Abraham and to his seed. The wording does not say, “and to seeds,” as though referring to many, but rather to one: “and to your seed,”* who is Christ.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="GAL 3:17"/>
    What I am saying is this: The Law, which came four hundred and thirty years later, does not cancel a covenant previously confirmed by God, so as to invalidate the promise.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="GAL 3:18"/>
    For if the inheritance is based on the Law, it is no longer based on a promise; but God graciously granted it to Abraham through a promise.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="GAL 3:19"/>
    Why then was the Law given? It was added because of transgressions, until the Seed to whom the promise was made should come. It was put into effect through angels by the hand of a mediator.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="GAL 3:20"/>
    Now a mediator does not represent only one party, but God is one.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="GAL 3:21"/>
    Is the Law, then, opposed to the promises of God? Absolutely not. For if a law had been given that could impart life, then righteousness would indeed come through the Law.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="GAL 3:22"/>
    But the Scripture has confined everything under the power of sin, so that what was promised, being based on faith in Jesus Christ, might be given to those who believe.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="GAL 3:23"/>
    Before faith came, we were held in custody under the Law, confined until the faith that was to be revealed arrived.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="GAL 3:24"/>
    In this way, the Law served as our guardian, leading us to Christ, so that we might be justified or declared righteous by faith.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="GAL 3:25"/>
    But now that faith has come, we are no longer under a guardian.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="GAL 3:26"/>
    For you are all sons of God through faith in Christ Jesus.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="GAL 3:27"/>
    For all of you who were baptised into Christ have clothed yourselves with Christ.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="GAL 3:28"/>
    There is neither Jew nor Greek, neither slave nor free, there is no male and female, for you are all one in Christ Jesus.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="GAL 3:29"/>
    And if you belong to Christ, then you are Abraham’s seed, heirs according to promise.
    <verse eid="GAL 3:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">8 Quoted from Gen. 12:3</para>
  <para style="p">10 Quoted from Deut. 27:26</para>
  <para style="p">11 Quoted from Hab. 2:4</para>
  <para style="p">12 Quoted from Lev. 18:5</para>
  <para style="p">13 Quoted from Deut. 21:23</para>
  <para style="p">16 Quoted from Gen. 12:7; 13:15</para>
  <chapter eid="GAL 3"/>
  <chapter number="4" style="c" sid="GAL 4"/>
  <para style="s1">Sons and Heirs</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="GAL 4:1"/>
    I say this: As long as the heir is a child, he is in no way different from a slave, even though he owns everything.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="GAL 4:2"/>
    He is under guardians and stewards until the time set by his father.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="GAL 4:3"/>
    In the same way, we were children, enslaved under the basic principles of the world.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="GAL 4:4"/>
    But when the full time had come, God sent His Son, born of a woman and born under the Law,
    <verse eid="GAL 4:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="GAL 4:5"/>
    in order to redeem those under the Law, so that we might receive the adoption as sons.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="GAL 4:6"/>
    Because you are sons, God sent the Spirit of His Son into our hearts, crying out, “Abba! Father!”
    <verse eid="GAL 4:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="GAL 4:7"/>
    Therefore, you are no longer a slave but a son, and if a son, then also an heir through God.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Paul’s Concern for the Galatians</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="GAL 4:8"/>
    At that time, when you did not know God, you were enslaved to things that are not gods by nature.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="GAL 4:9"/>
    But now, having come to know God—or rather, having been known by God—how can you turn back to the weak and worthless basic principles, to which you want to submit yourselves again?
    <verse eid="GAL 4:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="GAL 4:10"/>
    You observe days, months, seasons, and years.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="GAL 4:11"/>
    I am afraid for you, brothers and sisters, that my labour on your behalf has been in vain.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="GAL 4:12"/>
    I urge you, brothers and sisters, become like me, for I have become like you. You have done me no wrong.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="GAL 4:13"/>
    You know that it was because of a bodily weakness that I first preached the gospel to you.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="GAL 4:14"/>
    You did not despise or reject the trial of my physical condition, but you received me as an angel of God, even as Christ Jesus Himself.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="GAL 4:15"/>
    Where then is your blessing? I testify to you that if it were possible, you would have torn out your eyes and given them to me!
    <verse eid="GAL 4:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="GAL 4:16"/>
    So then, have I become your enemy by telling you the truth?
    <verse eid="GAL 4:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="GAL 4:17"/>
    They are eager for you, but not for good; they want to exclude you so that you may be eager for them.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="GAL 4:18"/>
    It is good to be zealous in a noble cause, and not only when I am present with you.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="GAL 4:19"/>
    My children, for whom I am again in the pains of childbirth until Christ is fully formed in you!
    <verse eid="GAL 4:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="GAL 4:20"/>
    I wish I could be present with you now and change my tone, because I am deeply perplexed about you.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Hagar and Sarah</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="GAL 4:21"/>
    Tell me, those of you who desire to live under the Law, do you not listen to what the Law says?
    <verse eid="GAL 4:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="GAL 4:22"/>
    For it is written that Abraham had two sons, one by the slave woman and one by the free woman.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="GAL 4:23"/>
    The son of the slave woman was born according to human descent, but the son of the free woman was born through the promise.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="GAL 4:24"/>
    These things are allegorical: The two women represent two covenants. One covenant is from Mount Sinai, bearing children for slavery, which is Hagar.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="GAL 4:25"/>
    Hagar corresponds to Mount Sinai in Arabia and represents the present Jerusalem, because she and her children are in slavery.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="GAL 4:26"/>
    But the Jerusalem above is free; she is our mother.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="GAL 4:27"/>
    For it is written:
    <verse eid="GAL 4:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">“Rejoice, O barren one who does not bear;</para>
  <para style="p">burst out and shout,</para>
  <para style="p">you who do not experience the pains of childbirth,</para>
  <para style="p">because many are the children of the desolate one,</para>
  <para style="p">more than those of the one who has a husband.”*</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="GAL 4:28"/>
    Now you, brothers and sisters, are children of promise, like Isaac.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="GAL 4:29"/>
    But just as the one born according to the flesh persecuted the one born according to the Spirit at that time, so it is now.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="GAL 4:30"/>
    However, what does the Scripture say? “Expel the slave woman and her son, for the son of the slave woman will never share the inheritance with the son of the free woman.”*
    <verse eid="GAL 4:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="GAL 4:31"/>
    Therefore, brothers and sisters, we are not children of the slave woman, but of the free woman.
    <verse eid="GAL 4:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">27 Quoted from Is. 54:1</para>
  <para style="p">30 Quoted from Gen. 21:10</para>
  <chapter eid="GAL 4"/>
  <chapter number="5" style="c" sid="GAL 5"/>
  <para style="s1">The Liberty Given by Christ</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="GAL 5:1"/>
    It is for freedom that Christ has set us free. Stand firm, therefore, and do not submit again to a yoke of slavery.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="GAL 5:2"/>
    Look! I, Paul, tell you that if you accept circumcision, Christ will benefit you nothing.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="GAL 5:3"/>
    I again testify to every man who chooses circumcision that he is obligated to obey the entire Law.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="GAL 5:4"/>
    You who are seeking to be justified or declared righteous by the Law have separated yourselves from Christ; you have fallen from grace.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="GAL 5:5"/>
    For through the Spirit, we eagerly wait by faith for the hope of righteousness.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="GAL 5:6"/>
    For in Christ Jesus, neither circumcision nor uncircumcision has any value, but only faith working through love.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="GAL 5:7"/>
    You were running a good race. Who hindered you from obeying the truth?
    <verse eid="GAL 5:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="GAL 5:8"/>
    This persuasion does not come from the One who calls you.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="GAL 5:9"/>
    A little yeast spreads through the whole batch of dough.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="GAL 5:10"/>
    I am confident in you, brothers and sisters, that you will remain steadfast in the Lord, and the one troubling you will face judgment, whoever he may be.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="GAL 5:11"/>
    Brothers, if I still preach circumcision, why am I still being persecuted? In that case, the offense of the cross has been nullified.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="GAL 5:12"/>
    I wish that those upsetting you would go so far as to mutilate themselves!
    <verse eid="GAL 5:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="GAL 5:13"/>
    For you were called to freedom, brothers and sisters. Only do not use your freedom as an opportunity for the flesh, but rather, through love, serve one another.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="GAL 5:14"/>
    For the entire Law is fulfilled in this one command: “You shall love your neighbour as yourself.”
    <verse eid="GAL 5:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="GAL 5:15"/>
    But if you bite and devour one another, be careful that you are not consumed by each other.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">Walking by the Spirit and Aligning with the Spirit</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="GAL 5:16"/>
    I say this: Live by the Spirit, and you will never fulfil the desire of the flesh.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="GAL 5:17"/>
    For the flesh desires what is opposed to the Spirit, and the Spirit desires what is opposed to the flesh. They are in conflict with each other, so that you are unable to do what you desire.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="GAL 5:18"/>
    But if you are led by the Spirit, you are not under the Law.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="GAL 5:19"/>
    Now the works of the flesh are obvious: sexual immorality, impurity, sensual indulgence,
    <verse eid="GAL 5:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="GAL 5:20"/>
    idolatry, sorcery, hostilities, strife, jealousy, outbursts of anger, selfish ambitions, rivalries, divisions,
    <verse eid="GAL 5:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="GAL 5:21"/>
    envy, drunkenness, orgies, and the like—I am warning you, as I warned you before, that those who practise such things will not inherit the kingdom of God.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="GAL 5:22"/>
    But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness,
    <verse eid="GAL 5:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="GAL 5:23"/>
    gentleness and self-control. Against such qualities there is no law.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="GAL 5:24"/>
    Those who belong to Christ Jesus have put the flesh to death, along with its passions and desires.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="GAL 5:25"/>
    Since we live by the Spirit, let us also follow the Spirit’s guidance.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="GAL 5:26"/>
    Let us not become conceited, provoking one another, or envying one another.
    <verse eid="GAL 5:26"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="GAL 5"/>
  <chapter number="6" style="c" sid="GAL 6"/>
  <para style="s1">Bearing One Another’s Burdens</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="GAL 6:1"/>
    Brothers and sisters, if a man is caught in any sin, you who are spiritual should restore him gently, being careful to watch yourself, so that you are not also tempted.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="GAL 6:2"/>
    Carry one another’s burdens, and in doing so, you will fulfil the law of Christ.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="GAL 6:3"/>
    For if anyone thinks he is something when he is nothing, he deceives himself.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="GAL 6:4"/>
    Let each one examine his own actions, and then he will have cause for pride in himself alone, and not in comparison with others.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="GAL 6:5"/>
    For each one will bear his own responsibility.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Sowing to the Spirit</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="GAL 6:6"/>
    Let the one who is taught the message share all good things with the one who teaches.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="GAL 6:7"/>
    Do not be deceived: God cannot be mocked. Whatever a man sows, he will also reap.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="GAL 6:8"/>
    Those who sow to satisfy their flesh will reap decay from the flesh, but those who sow to the Spirit will reap eternal life from the Spirit.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="GAL 6:9"/>
    Let us not grow weary in doing good, for in due time we will reap if we do not give up.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="GAL 6:10"/>
    Therefore, as we have opportunity, let us do good to all people, especially to those who belong to the household of faith.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Superiority of the New Creation Over Circumcision and Uncircumcision</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="GAL 6:11"/>
    See the large letters in which I have written this with my own hand!
    <verse eid="GAL 6:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="GAL 6:12"/>
    All who desire to make a favourable impression on the flesh—these people are insisting on circumcision—do so only to avoid persecution for the cross of Christ.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="GAL 6:13"/>
    For not even those who are circumcised obey the Law themselves; yet they want you circumcised so that they may boast in your flesh.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="GAL 6:14"/>
    But may it never be that I should boast except in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, through which the world has been crucified to me, and I to the world.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="GAL 6:15"/>
    For neither circumcision nor uncircumcision has any value; what matters is being a new creation.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="GAL 6:16"/>
    Peace and mercy be upon all who follow this rule, and upon the Israel of God.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Conclusion and Benediction</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="GAL 6:17"/>
    From now on, let no one cause trouble for me, for I bear on my body the marks of Jesus.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="GAL 6:18"/>
    May the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit, brothers. Amen.
    <verse eid="GAL 6:18"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="GAL 6"/>
</usx>
